## 8. API Design

### 8.1 Create Short URL

Endpoint:

> POST /api/v1/shorten

Request body:

~~~json
{
  "long_url": "https://example.com/products/123",
  "custom_alias": "summer-sale",
  "expires_at": "2027-01-01T00:00:00Z"
}
~~~

Successful response:

> 201 Created

~~~json
{
  "short_code": "summer-sale",
  "short_url": "https://short.url/summer-sale",
  "long_url": "https://example.com/products/123",
  "created_at": "2026-11-23T10:00:00Z",
  "expires_at": "2027-01-01T00:00:00Z"
}
~~~

A new short code can be created for every request, even when the long URL was shortened before.

This supports separate analytics for:

- Different users
- Different campaigns
- Different platforms
- Different expiration settings

---

### 8.2 Redirect Short URL

Endpoint:

> GET /{shortCode}

Example:

> GET /abc123

Successful response:

~~~http
HTTP/1.1 302 Found
Location: https://example.com/products/123
~~~

Possible failures:

- `404 Not Found`: Code does not exist, has expired or is inactive.
- `403 Forbidden`: Destination was blocked.
- `429 Too Many Requests`: Rate limit exceeded.
- `500 Internal Server Error`: Unexpected server failure.

---

### 8.3 Get Analytics

Endpoint:

> GET /api/v1/stats/{shortCode}

Example response:

~~~json
{
  "short_code": "abc123",
  "total_clicks": 15240,
  "unique_clicks": 10320,
  "countries": {
    "IN": 8000,
    "US": 4200
  },
  "devices": {
    "mobile": 9800,
    "desktop": 5440
  },
  "top_referrers": [
    "linkedin.com",
    "google.com"
  ]
}
~~~

The analytics API should read aggregated information instead of scanning all raw click events.

---

### 8.4 Bulk URL Creation

Endpoint:

> POST /api/v1/shorten/bulk

This endpoint is useful for:

- Marketing campaigns
- Enterprise customers
- Data imports
- Large URL collections

It should support:

- Authentication
- Batch-size limits
- Premium quotas
- Idempotency
- Asynchronous processing for very large batches

