## 16. Short-Code Generation Requirements

A short-code generation strategy should provide:

- Uniqueness
- Compact codes
- High throughput
- Low latency
- Horizontal scalability
- Collision prevention
- Custom alias compatibility

Common strategies:

1. Hashing
2. Counter with Base62
3. Distributed ID generation
4. Pre-generated code pool
5. Random code generation

---

## 17. Hashing Strategy

Process:

~~~text
Long URL
   |
   v
Hash Function
   |
   v
Truncate Hash
   |
   v
Short Code
~~~

Example:

> SHA-256(long URL) → first seven characters

### Problem

The complete hash is too long.

When the hash is truncated, different URLs may produce the same shortened value.

This is called a collision.

### Collision Solutions

- Use additional hash characters.
- Check the database before insertion.
- Add a random salt and retry.
- Append additional characters.
- Use a separate unique ID.

### Limitation

Hashing the same URL may produce the same short code.

That may be undesirable when separate campaign analytics are required.

### Conclusion

Hashing is possible, but pure truncated hashing is not the cleanest option when guaranteed uniqueness is required.

---

## 18. Counter and Base62 Strategy

Process:

1. Obtain a unique numeric ID.
2. Convert the ID to Base62.
3. Use the Base62 value as the short code.
4. Store the mapping.

Example:

~~~text
Numeric ID: 125
Base62 value: 21
Short URL: https://short.url/21
~~~

### Base62 Characters

Base62 uses:

- `0-9`: 10 characters
- `A-Z`: 26 characters
- `a-z`: 26 characters

Total:

> 10 + 26 + 26 = 62 characters

### Capacity

Seven Base62 characters provide:

> 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion combinations

### Advantages

- Collision-free if numeric IDs are unique
- Compact result
- Easy to understand
- Easy to implement
- Fast encoding

### Challenges

- A global counter can become a bottleneck.
- Sequential codes may be predictable.
- The central counter may become a single point of failure.
- Generated codes may conflict with custom aliases.

---

## 19. Scaling the Counter

### 19.1 Range Allocation

Each creation server receives a unique range.

~~~text
Server A: 1 to 1,000,000
Server B: 1,000,001 to 2,000,000
Server C: 2,000,001 to 3,000,000
~~~

Advantages:

- Servers do not request the next ID for every URL.
- Creation latency is reduced.
- The ID service receives fewer calls.
- Servers can generate IDs independently.

Trade-off:

- Some IDs may be unused when a server crashes.

Unused IDs are normally acceptable because the namespace is very large.

---

### 19.2 Distributed ID Generation

A Snowflake-style ID can combine:

~~~text
Timestamp + Region ID + Machine ID + Sequence Number
~~~

Conceptual structure:

~~~text
| timestamp | region ID | machine ID | sequence |
~~~

Why it works:

- Timestamp changes as time moves forward.
- Machine ID prevents conflicts between servers.
- Sequence number supports multiple IDs in the same millisecond.

Advantages:

- High throughput
- No central counter bottleneck
- Globally unique IDs
- Rough time ordering

Challenges:

- Clock synchronization
- Clock moving backward
- Machine ID management
- Region ID management
- Larger numeric IDs

The resulting numeric ID is encoded using Base62.

---

### 19.3 Pre-Generated Code Pool

A background service generates unique codes in advance.

Creation servers reserve unused codes from the pool.

Advantages:

- Very fast URL creation
- Codes can be verified before use
- Creation servers need less generation logic

Disadvantages:

- More operational complexity
- Pool refill management
- Handling reserved but unused codes
- Code-pool availability becomes important

---

## 20. Predictable Short Codes

Sequential numeric IDs encoded directly in Base62 may be predictable.

An attacker may try:

~~~text
abc100
abc101
abc102
abc103
~~~

Possible solutions:

- Apply a reversible permutation.
- XOR the numeric ID with a secret.
- Use Hashids-style obfuscation.
- Use a Feistel-network-based permutation.
- Generate random codes and check uniqueness.

Important:

Obfuscation is not authentication.

A private URL should require proper authorization instead of relying only on an unguessable short code.

---

## 21. Custom Alias Handling

Example custom alias:

> https://short.url/summer-sale

Validation rules:

- Allow only approved characters.
- Enforce minimum length.
- Enforce maximum length.
- Reject reserved words.
- Reject offensive aliases if required.
- Enforce uniqueness.
- Apply user-plan restrictions.

Reserved aliases may include:

- `api`
- `admin`
- `login`
- `support`
- `help`
- `billing`
- `health`
- `metrics`
- `static`

### Conflict Handling

If the alias already exists:

> Return `409 Conflict`.

### Race Condition

Suppose two users request `summer-sale` simultaneously.

An unsafe implementation:

~~~text
1. Check whether alias exists.
2. Alias appears available.
3. Insert alias.
~~~

Both requests may see that the alias is available.

Use:

- An atomic conditional insert
- A unique database constraint
- A transaction

Exactly one request succeeds.

The other receives `409 Conflict`.

Generated codes and custom aliases must use the same protected namespace or clearly separate namespaces.

