## 46. Interview Questions and Short Answers

### Why is the redirect path the hot path?

A URL is created once but may be clicked millions of times. Redirect traffic greatly exceeds creation traffic.

### Why is Base62 useful?

Base62 represents large numeric IDs using fewer URL-friendly characters.

### Why can truncated hashing cause collisions?

The complete hash has a large key space. Truncating it into a short code reduces the key space, allowing two URLs to produce the same code.

### Why use a 302 redirect?

It keeps the shortener in the request path, supporting analytics, expiration, destination changes and abuse blocking.

### Why not update the click count directly?

A popular link would cause many writes to one record or partition, creating contention and increased latency.

### Why should analytics be asynchronous?

Redirects require very low latency. Analytics enrichment and aggregation are slower and can tolerate delays.

### What is a hot key?

A key receiving much more traffic than other keys, potentially overloading a cache node or database partition.

### Why is caching essential?

Without caching, every redirect reaches the database. CDN, local cache and Redis absorb most read traffic.

### What happens if Redis fails?

The system uses CDN, local cache and controlled database fallback while circuit breakers and load shedding protect the database.

### How are custom alias conflicts handled?

Use an atomic conditional insert or unique constraint. One request succeeds and competing requests receive `409 Conflict`.

### How do we support global low-latency redirects?

Use CDN edge caching, regional redirect services, regional caches, replicated URL data and nearest-region routing.

### Should the same long URL always return the same short code?

Not necessarily. Separate codes provide separate analytics for different users and campaigns.

### Why is NoSQL useful for URL mappings?

The main access pattern is a simple key-value lookup by short code. NoSQL databases can scale this workload horizontally.

### Can analytics be delayed?

Yes. Analytics can usually be eventually consistent as long as redirects remain correct and fast.

